## 5. Database Design

The initial database design will consist of three primary entities.

### User

| Field | Description |
|---|---|
| id | Unique user identifier |
| username | User's username |
| email | User's email address |
| password | Hashed password |
| created_at | Account creation timestamp |

### BlogPost

| Field | Description |
|---|---|
| id | Unique post identifier |
| title | Blog post title |
| content | Main blog content |
| author_id | Reference to the user who created the post |
| status | Draft or published |
| created_at | Post creation timestamp |
| updated_at | Last modification timestamp |

### Comment

| Field | Description |
|---|---|
| id | Unique comment identifier |
| content | Comment text |
| author_id | Reference to the user who commented |
| post_id | Reference to the associated blog post |
| created_at | Comment creation timestamp |

### Entity Relationships

- One user can create multiple blog posts.
- One user can write multiple comments.
- One blog post can have multiple comments.
- Each blog post belongs to a single author.

```text
User (1) -------- (N) BlogPost
User (1) -------- (N) Comment
BlogPost (1) ---- (N) Comment
```

## 6. API Structure

The backend will expose REST APIs to support the platform's core functionality.

### Authentication APIs

| Method | Endpoint | Description |
|---|---|---|
| POST | `/api/auth/register/` | Register a new user |
| POST | `/api/auth/login/` | Authenticate a user |
| POST | `/api/auth/logout/` | Log out a user |

### Blog APIs

| Method | Endpoint | Description |
|---|---|---|
| GET | `/api/blogs/` | Retrieve all published blogs |
| GET | `/api/blogs/{id}/` | Retrieve a specific blog |
| POST | `/api/blogs/` | Create a new blog |
| PUT/PATCH | `/api/blogs/{id}/` | Update an existing blog |
| DELETE | `/api/blogs/{id}/` | Delete a blog |

### Comment APIs

| Method | Endpoint | Description |
|---|---|---|
| GET | `/api/blogs/{id}/comments/` | Retrieve comments for a blog |
| POST | `/api/blogs/{id}/comments/` | Add a comment |
| DELETE | `/api/comments/{id}/` | Delete a comment |

All write operations will require appropriate authentication and authorization.

## 7. Application Workflows

### User Registration and Authentication

1. A user submits registration details through the frontend.
2. The frontend sends the information to the backend.
3. The backend validates the information and securely stores the user's credentials.
4. During login, the backend verifies the credentials.
5. Upon successful authentication, the backend establishes an authenticated session or returns an authentication token.

### Blog Creation

1. An authenticated user opens the blog creation interface.
2. The user enters the title and content.
3. The frontend sends the data to the backend.
4. The backend validates the request and verifies the user's identity.
5. The blog post is stored in the database with the user as its author.
6. The newly created post becomes available according to its publication status.

### Blog Viewing

1. A user visits the blog listing page.
2. The frontend requests published blog posts from the backend.
3. The backend retrieves the relevant posts from the database.
4. The frontend displays the posts.
5. Selecting a post fetches and displays its complete content and associated comments.

## 8. Security and Authorization

The platform should follow basic security practices:

- Passwords must be securely hashed and never stored as plain text.
- Protected endpoints must require authentication.
- Users should only be allowed to modify or delete their own blog posts.
- Input validation should be performed on the backend.
- Appropriate permissions should be enforced for comment management.
- Sensitive configuration values should be stored in environment variables.

## 9. Future Enhancements

The architecture should allow additional functionality to be introduced later, such as:

- Blog categories and tags.
- Search and filtering.
- Pagination.
- Likes and bookmarks.
- Rich text or Markdown editor.
- Image and media uploads.
- Notifications.
- Admin dashboard.
- Caching and performance optimization.

## 10. Conclusion

This document establishes the initial architectural direction and functional scope of the Blog Platform.

The proposed structure is intentionally modular so that individual components can be developed, tested, and maintained independently.

The architecture and database design are preliminary and can evolve as implementation requirements become clearer.
